--expect
63 0 0 3 0 -4 7
--expect-why
DERIVED FROM THE FORMAT, field by field. Each is a bit-packed set of answers
so that one wrong refusal cannot be hidden by another going the other way.

  ok1 = 63   A WELL-FORMED TABLE, all six steps true (1+2+4+8+16+32):
             open a table of one column; two values for its name and type;
             the row count; one cell; and then the message is complete.
             Any step refusing drops its bit, so 63 names the whole sequence
             and not merely its end.

  ok2 = 0    A VALUE WHERE THE COUNT IS DUE. This is the property the marker
             exists for and the one a 32-bit mask destroyed: with the sign
             lost, the frame reads as an ordinary positive count and the
             value is ACCEPTED. Rust answered 0 and both ports answered 1
             until `fixnum64` existed.

  ok3 = 0    A ROW COUNT WHERE NO TABLE IS OPEN. The other half of the same
             rule: a count goes only where a count is due, so its four bytes
             can never be read as a tag and an id.

  ok4 = 3    AN EMPTY TABLE completes on its row count (1) and reports
             complete (2). Zero cells is the case where the writer finishes
             on the count itself, with no cell following it.

  ok5 = 0    THE PRODUCT BOUND. Two columns and 2^40 rows is 2^41 cells,
             past `MAX_CELLS`, so the count is refused rather than wrapping.
             An unchecked product wraps into the marker space -- which is the
             same hole one level down.

  marker = -4  THE SIGN ITSELF, read straight out of the frame. A table of
             THREE columns stores `-ncols - 1`, and `-3 - 1` is -4. It is the
             frame under the `2 * ncols` pairs frame, hence `deep - 2`.
             Negative is the whole encoding of "a count is due", so this
             field fails on any target that widens a signed 64-bit value
             through a 32-bit mask: 0xFFFFFFFC read unsigned is 4294967292.
             -4 and 4294967292 cannot be confused for a formatting
             difference.

  ok7 = 7    A BUFFER THAT CANNOT GROW, and all three bits matter
             (1+2+4). `b-conj` answers NIL when `tb-flush` cannot allocate a
             fresh tail, and the driver reaches that with a switch rather than
             by exhausting a heap it does not have.

             `1` the append is REFUSED. On its own this bit is weak: it is the
                 obvious thing to assert and the broken version can be made to
                 pass it by returning false after the damage is done.

             `2` AND `WR_BUF` IS UNTOUCHED. This is the bit the bug would
                 fail. The first version stored the NIL and answered true; a
                 half-fix that answers false and still stores it leaves a
                 writer whose buffer is nil, which is the same corpse by a
                 quieter route. Comparing the slot against what it held
                 BEFORE the refusal is what separates "declined" from
                 "destroyed", and nothing else here does.

             `4` AND THE WRITER STILL WORKS. A refusal is not a kill: the
                 writer was never finished, so once the buffer can grow the
                 byte goes on. Without this field, `wire-byte` could satisfy
                 the first two by marking the writer dead -- which would turn
                 a transient allocation failure into a permanently broken
                 writer, and a retry into a second, more confusing error.
